      "text": "To simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\), we start by using the property of exponents that allows us to separate the terms inside the fourth root into separate factors raised to the fourth root of each factor. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^7} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{3^3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the fourth root of each term separately. For \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7}\\), we can rewrite it as:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7} = 2^{7/4} = 2^{6/4 + 1/4} = 2^{\\frac{6}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}} = 2^{\\frac{7}{4}}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(\\sqrt[4]{3^3}\\), we can directly apply the fourth root to the whole term:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{3^3} = 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nNow we can combine these results:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = 2^{\\frac{7}{4}} \\cdot 3^{\\frac{3}{4}}\n\\]\n\nThis expression is already in the form \\(a\\sqrt[4]{b}\\), where \\(a = 2^{\\frac{7}{4}} \\cdot 3^{\\frac{3}{4}}\\) and \\(b = 1\\). However, we need to express \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) in whole numbers. Notice that \\(2^{\\frac{7}{4}} = 2^{3 + \\frac{1}{4}} = 2^3 \\cdot 2^{\\frac{1}{4}} = 8 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2}\\), and \\(3^{\\frac{3}{4}}\\) remains as it is. Therefore, we can rewrite \\(a\\) as:\n\n\\[\na = 8 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2} \\cdot 3^{\\frac{3}{4}} = 8 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2 \\cdot 3^3} = 8 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2 \\cdot 27} = 8 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{54}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(54 = 2 \\cdot 3^3\\), we have:\n\n\\[\na = 8 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2} = 24 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{2}}{\\sqrt[4]{2}} = 24 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^2} = 24 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{4} = 24 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{4} = 24 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{4} = 24 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\na = 8 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{4} = 24\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(a = 24\\) and \\(b = 4\\), and therefore:\n\n\\[\na + b = 24 + 4 = 28\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{28}\n\\]",
